      "writtenOn": "2026-04-27T20:13:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e So this check was redundant, because get_vdo.vdo_req.num_vdos is defined as only 3 bits so max value is 7. I can add check for this but compiler already enforce check for num_vdos.\n\nAck\n\n\u003e I don\u0027t think we write up to vdo_index. We write the VDO at specific index meaning we can have VDOs arr as [0,0,0,0x1111,0,0] but this is what we want.\n\u003e\n\u003e I also check vdo_index \u003c RTS54XX_VDO_MAX_NUM so it is not possible to read outside of arr.\n\nWhat I mean is that if the index in element 2 is invalid, this function will read a store VDOs for the valid indices in elements 0 and 1 and then return an error. I would have expected that if any index was invalid, it would return an error, not modify `vdo` at all, and set `byte_count` to 0 or something.\n\nBut the behavior of the emulator should match the actual chip, which is why I asked about the observed behavior.\n\nIt looks like your test is now checking that `vdo` is not modified with an invalid index, so maybe I\u0027m still missing something here.",
      "parentUuid": "5d8f1b5e_7fa2f8f6",
      "revId": "b9914d391a6d94f86ba6e2502f42b79f068f7f72",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
